\import{swarm-macros}

\date{2025-11-20T16:44:03Z}
\title{Capability set expressions}

\p{Since types can be capability-polymorphic, it's not enough to just
keep track of literal capability sets; we need to keep track of variables that can stand for unknown capability sets, which can in turn combine with other capability sets.  A capability set expression can be:

\ul{
  \li{the empty capability set #{\varnothing},}
  \li{a capability set variable #{\delta},}
  \li{a singleton literal capability set, such as #{\{\Exc{\sf{if}}\}}, or}
  \li{the union of two capability set expressions, #{\Delta_1 \cup \Delta_2},}
}

but quotiented by the assumption that #{\varnothing} is the identity for #{\cup}, and #{\cup} is associative, commutative and idempotent.  For example, #{(\varnothing \cup \delta) \cup (\{\sf{Lambda}\} \cup \delta) = \delta \cup \{\sf{Lambda}\}}.  We also write set literals like #{\{\Exc{\sf{if}}, \sf{Lambda}\}} as an abbreviation for #{\{\Exc{\sf{if}}\} \cup \{\sf{Lambda}\}}.
}
